  "nameStory": "",
  "description": "Neofavolus suavissimus, commonly known as the Delicate Polypore, is a small bracket fungus characterized by its delicate, fan-shaped fruiting bodies. This species is a wood-decomposer found on dead hardwood substrates throughout New England.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Pore Surface",
      "description": "Angular to hexagonal pores on the underside that are relatively large and radiate from the point of attachment",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Size and Shape",
      "description": "Small, thin, fan or shelf-shaped fruiting bodies, typically 1-4 cm wide with a delicate appearance",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Color",
      "description": "White to cream-colored upper surface with a white pore surface that may yellow with age",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "Neofavolus suavissimus grows on dead hardwood branches and logs, preferring moist conditions in forests and wooded areas throughout New England.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "",
    "summer": "",
    "fall": "",
    "winter": ""
  },
  "funFact": "Despite its delicate appearance, this polypore plays an important ecological role as a wood-decay fungus, helping to break down dead plant material and return nutrients to the forest ecosystem.",
